# Prefer real types over ad-hoc shapes + casts

When a function operates on a recursive or already-modeled structure, its
parameter types must mirror the actual structure it expects — not a
loosely-typed stand-in that requires an internal cast to be usable.

If you find yourself writing `as typeof X` (or any cast) to make a narrowed
inline type behave like the real one, that's a signal the parameter type is
wrong. Fix the type, don't cast around it.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — inline shape with `unknown[][]` children can't actually recurse;
// requires a cast to "restore" the real type when descending.
const walk = (
  columns: { header: { deleted: boolean }; meta: { index: number }; children: unknown[][] }[][],
) => {
  for (const node of columns.flat()) {
    if (!node.header.deleted) walk(node.children as typeof columns);
  }
};

// ✅ GOOD — use the library's real recursive type; `node.children` is already
// that same type, so no cast is needed to recurse.
const walk = (
  columns: RecursiveFormItem<TypeNames, Params, MetaDom<SIndexed>>[][],
) => {
  for (const node of columns.flat()) {
    if (!node.header.deleted) walk(node.children);
  }
};
```

Guidelines:

- Search for an existing type (e.g. `RecursiveFormItem`, `SectionWithItems`)
  before inventing a local inline object type for a function parameter.
- A parameter typed to accept `X[]` (or `unknown[]`) should genuinely be able
  to handle any value of type `X` — if the function body assumes a narrower
  shape, that narrower shape belongs in the parameter type.
- Prefer generic parameters matching the module's existing generics
  (`TypeNames`, `Params`, `Meta`, `SectionConfig`, …) over hardcoding concrete
  field lists.
